A **weak reference** points to an object without keeping it alive. If only weak references remain, the GC may collect the object, and the weak reference then reports "gone".

Go has them since **1.24**: the `weak` package.

```go
wp := weak.Make(obj)     // obj is *T
// ...
if p := wp.Value(); p != nil {
	use(p) // still alive; p is a strong pointer again
} else {
	// collected
}
```

Weak pointers made from the same pointer compare equal, so they work as map keys.

Uses: **canonicalization/interning caches** (the `unique` package, 1.23, is built on this), memoization caches that shouldn't pin memory, and observer registries. Pair them with `runtime.AddCleanup` (1.24, the preferred replacement for `SetFinalizer`) to delete the stale map entry when the object dies.

Gotchas: `Value()` can return nil at any moment after the last strong reference disappears, so always check it. Cleanups run asynchronously on a separate goroutine and aren't guaranteed to run before exit. Don't use weak pointers as a general cache-eviction policy; they drop entries at GC time, not by LRU or size.
